"## Entity linking and hyperlinks in Wikipedia used for evidence retrieval and claim verification: role of entity annotations in document retrieval, multi-hop evidence, and datasets like FEVER, KILT, and entity-centric benchmarks\n# Entity Linking & Wikipedia Hyperlinks in Evidence Retrieval and Claim Verification\n\n*Note: no live search tool is available in this session; this summary is compiled from my knowledge base (current to early 2025). Citation details should be verified before use.*\n\n## 1. Why Wikipedia hyperlinks matter\n\n- **Distant supervision for entity linking (EL):** anchor text → target-page links yield millions of (mention, entity) pairs, the basis for classic and modern EL datasets and models — Milne & Witten's link-based linking and relatedness measures [1], AIDA CoNLL [2], TAGME [3], zero-shot EL [4], BLINK [5], GENRE [6], LUKE [7], and Entities-as-Experts [8].\n- **Alias/redirect tables:** anchor statistics supply the \"commonness\" prior and name variants used to map claim mentions to page titles.\n- **Hyperlink graph as structure:** link-based semantic relatedness [1] and the link graph itself provide edges for traversal, expansion, and multi-hop reasoning.\n\n## 2. Role of entity annotations in document retrieval\n\n- **FEVER pipeline:** document retrieval → sentence retrieval → entailment [9]. The original baseline retrieved documents via Wikipedia search over claim entities; most strong systems annotate claim entities first.\n- **Entity-based retrieval wins:** UKP-Athene (FEVER 2018 shared-task winner) combined entity linking with Wikipedia search and TF-IDF retrieval [10]; GEAKO/MAaN used entity-linking-based retrieval plus one-hop expansion over page hyperlinks to catch multi-page evidence [11].\n- **EL as retrieval infrastructure:** BLINK and GENRE frame EL itself as dense/autoregressive retrieval over Wikipedia, sharing machinery with passage retrieval [5][6]; entity-augmented ranking (e.g., EDRM's entity–duet model) injects entities into neural IR [12]; REL provides efficient contextualized linking for such pipelines [13].\n- **Known failure mode:** dense retrievers underperform on entity-centric queries (EntityQuestions benchmark) [14], motivating hybrid entity+dense retrieval.\n\n## 3. Multi-hop evidence\n\n- **Datasets built from hyperlinks:** WikiHop was constructed directly from hyperlink chains between articles [15]; 2WikiMultihopQA combines hyperlinks with infobox triples, requiring entity-relation reasoning paths as evidence [16]; HotpotQA's supporting facts span two linked pages [17]; MuSiQue tests compositional multi-hop [18].\n- **Entity-graph retrievers:** DFGN builds an entity graph across documents with hyperlink edges for HotpotQA [19]; PathRetriever traverses the Wikipedia hyperlink graph to learn reasoning paths [20]; HopRetriever performs entity-based hop retrieval specifically for **multi-hop fact verification** [21]; DrKIT retrieves over an entity–relation index [22].\n- **Entity-supervised pretraining** (LUKE, Entities as Experts, ERNIE/KnowBERT) improves evidence selection and EL jointly [7][8].\n\n## 4. Benchmarks\n\n- **FEVER** [9]: 185k claims; SUPPORTS/REFUTES/NEI against Wikipedia; evidence = page+sentence provenance; entity linking is the standard first-stage retrieval tool. FEVEROUS extends to structured (tables/lists) evidence [23].\n- **KILT** [24]: unified benchmark on a 5.9M-page Wikipedia snapshot requiring **provenance** (R-precision/recall) for all tasks — fact-checking (FEVER), multi-hop QA (HotpotQA), slot filling (T-REx, aligned to Wikidata triples via EL [25]), and EL subtasks (Aidayago2/AidaBench, WNED-Wiki/CWEB) derived from Wikipedia links. KILT reported that entity linking serves as a useful auxiliary task for provenance quality.\n- **Entity-centric benchmarks:** AIDA CoNLL [2], WNED, Mewsli-X (cross-lingual) [26], GERBIL evaluation framework [27], EntityQuestions (retrieval) [14], CREAK (commonsense claims about entities) [28], VitaminC (verification robust to entity/number swaps via Wikipedia revisions) [29].\n\n## 5. Practical findings and limitations\n\n- Entity annotations act as (a) query-side anchors for title-based document retrieval, (b) alias/redirect expansion, (c) graph edges for hop retrieval, and (d) stable provenance anchors linking claims to evidence.\n- Failure modes: claims with **no named entities** (common in FEVER NEI/refuted classes) defeat entity-based retrieval; disambiguation errors cascade into wrong-page retrieval; hyperlink sparsity hurts long-tail entities; entity linking on short claims (few context words) is harder than on documents.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Milne & Witten 2008/2013, link-based EL and Wikipedia link-based relatedness (WLM)\n[2] Hoffart et al. 2011, AIDA CoNLL-YAGO (EMNLP)\n[3] Ferragina & Scaiella 2010, TAGME (IJCAI)\n[4] Logeswaran et al. 2019, Zero-shot Entity Linking (NAACL)\n[5] Wu et al. 2020, BLINK (EMNLP)\n[6] De Cao et al. 2021, GENRE (ICLR)\n[7] Yamada et al. 2020, LUKE (EMNLP)\n[8] Févry et al. 2020, Entities as Experts (EMNLP)\n[9] Thorne et al. 2018, FEVER (NAACL)\n[10] Hanselowski et al. 2018, UKP-Athene (FEVER workshop)\n[11] Nie et al. 2019, GEAKO/Multi-hop Attention Networks (arXiv)\n[12] Liu et al. 2018, Entity-Duet Neural Ranking (KDD)\n[13] van Hulst et al. 2020, REL (EMNLP Findings)\n[14] Sciavolino et al. 2021, EntityQuestions (EMNLP)\n[15] Welbl et al. 2017, WikiHop (TACL)\n[16] Ho et al. 2020, 2WikiMultihopQA (COLING)\n[17] Yang et al. 2018, HotpotQA (EMNLP)\n[18] Trivedi et al. 2022, MuSiQue (TACL)\n[19] Qiu et al. 2019, DFGN (ACL)\n[20] Asai et al. 2020, PathRetriever (ICLR)\n[21] Li et al. 2021, HopRetriever (ACL)\n[22] Dhingra et al. 2020, DrKIT (ICLR)\n[23] Aly et al. 2021, FEVEROUS (NeurIPS)\n[24] Petroni et al. 2021, KILT (NAACL)\n[25] Elsahar et al. 2018, T-REx (ACL)\n[26] Botha et al. 2020, Mewsli-X (ACL)\n[27] Usbeck et al. 2015, GERBIL\n[28] Onoe et al. 2021, CREAK (NeurIPS)\n[29] Schuster et al. 2021, VitaminC (NeurIPS)"